WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
 So now we're going to start learning

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.980
 the actual Python language.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.020
 So what do we say?

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.220
 You can think of this as almost like writing,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.480
 almost like writing a story.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.560
 We're gonna start with a basic vocabulary.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.800
 We're gonna talk a little bit about lines or sentences.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.260
 And then we're gonna start talking about

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.780
 how to put those sentences together

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.220
 to make a coherent paragraph, as it were.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.540
 And you just have to accept the fact

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.060
 that when I start teaching you this stuff,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.600
 it's not gonna make sense for about six or seven

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.020
 more chapters, and so just sort of bear with me,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.100
 except, I mean, I remember when I first learned,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.700
 it went from me confused, confused, confused, confused,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.700
 confused, holy mackerel, this is awesome.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.960
 And so I expect many of you will go through that same thing.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.440
 So just learn the first parts, accept the fact

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.660
 that it doesn't necessarily make sense in a big picture,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.620
 and just bear with us, okay?

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.020
 So we'll start with vocabulary, we'll start to make sentences,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.700
 and then we'll have little short stories and paragraphs, okay?

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.820
 And so this is a short story

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.620
 about how to count the words in Python.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.000
 It's got a couple of paragraphs,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.120
 and we are going to look at all of this stuff eventually.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.580
 So we start with a set of reserved words.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.380
 And what are reserved words?

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.020
 Well, they're words that Python expects

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.300
 when you use these words that they're gonna mean

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.340
 exactly what Python expects to mean.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.140
 And what it really means is you're not allowed

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.160
 to use them for any other purpose than the purpose

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.060
 that Python wants, it's sort of part of the contract.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.060
 It's like when you have a dog, and you go,

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.940
 what did you think of that television program?

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.580
 And the dog has no idea what you're saying,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.820
 and then you say, do you wanna wait until Saturday

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.860
 to go to the veterinarian?

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.500
 And the dog still doesn't know what you say,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.740
 and then you go like, how would you like to take a walk?

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.020
 And then the dog goes, walk, I know what that means,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.460
 and then hits the door, right?

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.760
 And so the way the dog sees you is blah, blah, blah,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.700
 blah, walk, blah, blah, blah, food,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.060
 blah, blah, blah, blah, treat, blah, blah, blah, walk.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.780
 That's kind of how Python looks at these reserved words.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.100
 When you say class, it goes class, oh, I know what that means.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.700
 Now, if I say zap, it's like, oh, zap something

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.860
 that you get to decide, or it's maybe a variable name.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.380
 So reserved words are simply words that,

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.820
 when you use these words in Python,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.900
 and there's only a few of 'em, like and, or del,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.660
 or if, maybe, pass, maybe, in.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.360
 A lot of these, you won't end up using them,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.500
 it's just these are reserved for Python

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.540
 and part of the Python vocabulary.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.720
 This is Python vocabulary.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.820
 Now, when we move from words to sentences,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.380
 you see that Python is a series of lines.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.980
 A Python program is a series of statements.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.580
 They have an order because the computer wants to know

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.180
 what next, what next, what next.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.500
 So what next is start at the beginning.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.680
 So I already talked about an assignment statement

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.940
 that basically says x equals two.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.540
 This is not a mathematical statement.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.740
 This is a directive to say, take this variable two,

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.500
 this value two, this constant two,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.140
 and stick it in a location in your memory

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.060
 and remember that I asked you to name it x.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.420
 X is a variable, something you made up.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.780
 You chose that, but it's Python's job to remember it.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.700
 So this says, go, whatever that x is,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.760
 there's a two in there, now pull that x back out,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.820
 add two to it, which makes it four,

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.220
 and stick it back in x, and so that makes this a four.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.700
 So x is a four.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.900
 And print x says, go look up that thing

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.660
 that was an x and print it out.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.980
 And so these are like, each line has something to it.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.340
 I'm using a reserved word.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.620
 Well, actually, that's a function,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.080
 but it's a reserved word, too.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.380
 And so there's reserved words and all these things,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.140
 and you combine these, there are operators.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.280
 Plus is an operator, equals is an operator.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.700
 These things do things,

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.020
 and we'll learn all this stuff in time.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.580
 So the basic building blocks of lines of Python.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.220
 Now, as we take these lines of Python and build them up,

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.580
 we end up making paragraphs, programming in paragraphs.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.780
 And so one of the things that it's important is

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.220
 I showed you how to do interactive Python,

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.420
 so you just type Python, and you type a statement,

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.540
 and a statement, and a statement.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.900
 Those get really tiring after about three or four lines

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.540
 of Python, because you start making mistakes,

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.420
 and you have to start over.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.700
 So the better thing to do is to,

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.980
 as your program gets a little larger, to write a script.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.160
 Put your Python instructions in a file,

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.120
 and then tell Python to read from the file,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.340
 and then run the script as it's entered in that file.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.080
 We tend to name these files with .py,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.900
 and I've got a series of videos that you can watch

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.100
 to figure out how this all works.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.660
 Like I said, you can type interactively to Python,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.340
 and it's a great way to experiment with Python,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.840
 check to see if a statement does what you think it does,

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.420
 but script is the way,

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.240
 after we are past one or two lines of code,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.660
 we write it in files and then run it separately.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.460
 So there are a couple of basic patterns,

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.940
 and it's really important to understand

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.500
 each of these patterns, and like I said,

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.500
 we'll teach you these patterns separately,

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.460
 and then we'll combine them together.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.940
 And when you combine them together is when you say,

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.580
 oh, that's what a program is.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.540
 So you have to suspend disbelief.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.220
 We have a couple of different patterns.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.240
 One is a sequence of steps.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.780
 Do this, then do this, then do this.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.040
 Conditional is like skipping something.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.220
 Repeated does it over and over and over again.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.500
 Computers are really good at repeating stuff.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.180
 Much better than people, people get tired

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.180
 going over and over doing the same thing.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.260
 And then we have store and repeated steps as well.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.500
 And so if we take a look at this,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.840
 and we take a look at a Python program,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.680
 this is a piece of code, this is a little script,

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.480
 if you type this into a code,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.740
 take this code, Python code, into a file and ran it,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.420
 it starts at the beginning, and then it goes to the next line

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.580
 and the next line and the next line,

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.640
 and Python executes the scripts as you write them.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.900
 So it says stick a variable, find a place called,

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.660
 in your memory called x, stick two into that, okay,

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.340
 then go to the next one, print that out,

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.240
 so the program's producing output.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.220
 Now go read x and add two to it and stick it back in x,

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.380
 so x is four, then print that.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.780
 This side over here, this is called a flowchart.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.340
 I'm not gonna make draw flowcharts,

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.020
 I'm only gonna draw them a few times

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.760
 in ways that I think will help you.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.620
 But you can think of it as Python,

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.900
 when it finishes something, it goes on to the next one,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.340
 unless you tell it otherwise.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:39.860
 Finishes this, goes on to the next one.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.740
 Finishes this, goes on to the next one.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.020
 Finishes this, and now the program is all done.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:46.660
 And so that's sequential steps.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.420
 You just type 'em in, Python runs it.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.420
 They're important, but sort of uninteresting,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.380
 'cause you can only get so far,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.900
 and you can't really make them intelligent

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.800
 'cause it's always gonna do the next one.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.100
 So the next thing we do is what are called conditional steps,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.660
 and this is where it starts to get intelligent.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.000
 I mean, where you are able to encode your brain

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.000
 into the computer like, oh, wait a sec,

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.340
 let's only do this step if something is true.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.820
 And the syntax that we tend to use here

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.500
 is the reserve word if, if, okay?

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.000
 And so the if is like a little fork in the road.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.060
 You can go one way, or you can go another way,

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.660
 and you're asking a question.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:28.760
 So inside the if statement, right here,

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.460
 there is a question saying, is x less than 10?

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.220
 That revolves, resolves to a true or false.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.720
 If it's less than 10, that's true.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.620
 If it's greater than 10, it's false.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:43.000
 And so then what we do is, if it's less than 10,

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.500
 we have this indented block of code.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.060
 There's also this colon that tells us

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.900
 we're in the beginning of an indented block of code.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.720
 And so what it basically says is,

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.040
 if this is true, run that code.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.740
 If it's false, skip that code.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.560
 So it can either run it or skip it,

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:59.740
 depending on this question that's being asked.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:00.820
 Now, if you look at this code,

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.780
 it's pretty obvious what's going on.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.200
 It comes down, x is five, if x is less than 10, that's true.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.080
 So it runs this code and prints out smaller.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.020
 And then it comes back here, deindents,

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.460
 the next basic sequential.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.600
 This ends up being kind of a block.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.760
 If x is greater than 20, this turns out to be false,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.040
 because x is five, and so it skips this.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.800
 So the bigger never comes out,

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.320
 and then it continues on, and then prints funny.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.480
 So it comes in, runs this, skips this, and then finishes.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.060
 So here is the last one we'll talk about,

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:37.900
 the repeated steps.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.680
 We'll get back to store and retrieve later,

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.480
 but for now we're just gonna talk about three of the four.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.380
 This is another program, and the key is is that

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:52.020
 we're gonna use this same choice where we're gonna go in,

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.640
 but then we're gonna run for a while,

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.320
 and then we'll have an exit condition where we get out.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.960
 So this is repeated over and over and over and over again.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.460
 And this is the essence of how we make computers

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.300
 do things that are seemingly difficult,

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.560
 while they're more naturally difficult for people.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.880
 And so how do we encode this notion

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.260
 that we wanna do something, not forever, but for a while?

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.620
 How do we encode that notion?

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.640
 And so we do it in this way.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.580
 So we have our statement, sequentially go to this while,

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.640
 while is a keyword, and it's asking another question

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:26.920
 that's a true/false question.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.780
 Is n greater than zero?

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.340
 I read this as, as long as n remains greater than zero,

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.060
 keep doing this indented block.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.340
 And you have a colon at the end,

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.980
 and then you have two lines of code that's indented.

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.880
 So that tells us what the loop is,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.020
 and then this is now deindented.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.560
 And so it comes in, and if this is true,

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.420
 if this is true, if this is true, it runs these two lines.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.660
 Prints out n, n is five, and then it says

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.780
 n equals n minus one, which makes n be four,

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:57.620
 and it goes back up, and it goes up

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:59.340
 and it asks this question again.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.140
 Is n greater than zero?

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.940
 If it is, continue on, and prints four,

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:05.900
 and then subtracts it, and it does that,

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.340
 four, three, two, and prints out one, then it comes up.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.420
 And now, after this, n is now zero, n is now zero,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.500
 and n is no longer greater than zero,

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.280
 so it takes sort of the exit ramp and goes down here.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.580
 So it takes the exit ramp and goes to here,

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.420
 and runs the next line.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.740
 Now, we're gonna cover all this again.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.200
 So I'm just trying to give you the big picture,

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:31.660
 next couple of chapters, we're gonna hit

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.440
 all these things again, and we're gonna hit them

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.460
 in much more detail with a lot better information.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:39.760
 This is now sort of like combining these.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.920
 And again, I don't want you to really know this stuff,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.600
 just you will know this in a couple of weeks,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.860
 you will see this program again.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.800
 But this shows you how we combine those patterns

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.960
 of repeated, sequential, and conditional together.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:00.440
 So this is a bit of sequential code, comes in here,

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.080
 runs this, which happens to ask for a file name,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.440
 then it opens the file, it creates a data structure

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.800
 called a dictionary, this is all sequential.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.120
 Now, the four is another form of loops,

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:11.960
 so this is gonna loop for a while,

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.380
 and then this is, within a loop,

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:16.060
 we can even have two indents, and that's another loop,

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:19.120
 so these are like repeated, and then it goes,

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.140
 it dows down to the next sequential bit,

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.940
 then it does this, here's another loop, it's gonna run,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.920
 and then here's a conditional, it's gonna run,

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.160
 and then when it's all done, we print out the last thing.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:32.240
 And this is, of course, is the program that does,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:36.820
 you know, the, it figures out the most common word

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.580
 and prints that most common word out.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:42.960
 And so this is a Python short story, it reads some data,

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.220
 it reads the name of a file, it opens that file,

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.820
 it talks about how to make a histogram,

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.500
 and then it looks through for the most common word.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.200
 So don't worry too much about this.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.340
 Over the next couple weeks, we'll fill in the pieces

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.140
 so that you absolutely understand

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.520
 every single line of this code.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:04.980
 So, this is a quick overview of chapter one.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:08.860
 Stick with us, you realize, it will be chapter seven

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:10.780
 before this makes too much sense.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:13.860
 You really have to trust that you are learning

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.980
 important things, and that it all makes sense

